---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Colophon · ${site.name}`}
  description="How kj5irq.radio is built: Astro, three self-hosted fonts, one accent color, no tracking."
>
  <article class="prose colophon">
    <header>
      <p class="label">Colophon</p>
      <h1>How this site is built</h1>
    </header>

    <p>
      Static pages built with <a href="https://astro.build" rel="noopener">Astro</a>, written in
      Markdown, hosted on GitHub Pages behind Cloudflare DNS. The source is public in
      <a href="https://github.com/KJ5IRQ/kj5irq.radio" rel="noopener">the repo</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Type is Source Serif 4 for words, with IBM Plex Mono for data and Courier Prime for the
      front page's small type, all self-hosted. The palette is cream paper, warm ink, and one
      accent: Ford Field Green.
    </p>
    <p>
      No analytics, no cookies, no tracking of any kind, and no code loaded from anyone else.
      Three things here run JavaScript: the dateline clock, the weather line, which reads our own
      Worker on wx.kj5irq.radio, and the theme toggle. With scripts off the page still reads,
      showing the weather as it stood when the site was built.
    </p>
    <p>
      Statuses and dates on project pages are real and maintained by hand. If something says
      active, it is running; if it says archived, it retired honestly.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the sections are named this</h2>
    <p>
      None of these is a synonym. Each is the word its trade uses for the thing the section is: a
      ledger is a record, an aerial is an antenna, a bookplate says whose book it is, and a
      colophon says how a book was made.
    </p>
    <dl class="names">
      <dt>Ledger</dt>
      <dd>What I run and what state it is in, kept current by hand.</dd>
      <dt>Aerial</dt>
      <dd>The antenna, and the station behind it. The part that actually radiates.</dd>
      <dt>Recorded nets</dt>
      <dd>Under Aerial. Appearances on other people's nets, with the dates and the recordings.</dd>
      <dt>Bookplate</dt>
      <dd>The printed mark inside a cover that says whose book this is. Here, mine.</dd>
      <dt>Marginalia</dt>
      <dd>Notes in the margins. The section appears once there is something in it.</dd>
      <dt>Colophon</dt>
      <dd>This page. Where a book explains how it was made.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .colophon header {
    padding-block: var(--space-8) var(--space-2);
  }

  .colophon {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }

  .names {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: max-content minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-3) var(--space-6);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }

  .names dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--size-meta);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
    padding-top: 3px;
  }

  .names dd {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  /* Two columns need room. Below that the name goes above its line, which is
     how the rest of the site behaves on a phone. */
  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .names {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-1);
    }

    .names dd {
      margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    }
  }
</style>
